= Electron Configuration

All physical/chemical properties of elements are ultimately based on electron configurations.

Three periodic atomic properties that directly influence electron configurations:
- Atomic size
- lonization energy
- IE Electron affinity
- EA lonic radius
- Metallic behavior

== Atomic Radius: Main Group Elements

- Down a group, atomic radius increases
  - $n$ increases, $Z_"eff" ~ "constant"$
  - outer electrons = further from nucleus
  - additional inner electron shield outer electrons
- Across a period, atomic size decreases
  - $n ~ "constant"$, $Z_"eff"$ increases
  - outer electrons pulled closer to nucleus shielding
  - $~ "constant"$ (no additional inner electrons)

== Ionization Energy

Ionization energy: IE - measure of ease with which atom gives up electrons

- $"IE"_1 < "IE"_2 < "IE"_3$
- Atoms with low IE tend to form cations during rxn (e.g., #ce[Cs] metal).
- Atoms with high IE (except noble gases) tend to form anions (e.g., #ce[Cl]).

Trends in IE:
- Down a group, IE decreases, $n$ increases
- Across a period, IE increases, $Z_"eff"$ increases

Difficulty of removing electrons - Valence electrons are easier to remove than core electrons

== Electron Affinity

Electron Affinity (EA) = energy change (kJ/mol) needed to add one mol of electrons to one mole of gaseous atoms or ions

$
  & ce("X(g) + e- => X-(g)") quad Delta E_"rxn" = "EA"_1 < 0 "(Usually, but not always)" \
  & ce("X-(g) + e- => X^(2-)(g)") quad Delta E_"rxn" = "EA"_2 > 0 "(always)"
$

- Energy may be absorbed or released
- Trends are not regular, with many exceptions

Trends in EA:
- Down a group, EA decreases (less negative/exothermic), $n$ increases
- Across a period, EA increases (more negative/exothermic), $Z_"eff"$ increases

Exceptions:
- Since a half-filled "p" subshell is more stable, carbon has a greater affinity for an electron than nitrogen
- 2A has a full "s" subshell, so it has a lower EA (less negative) than 1A

== Ions

=== Ion Formation: Electron Affinity

- Atoms with a *low EA* tend to form cations
- Atoms with a *high EA* tend to form anions (except noble gases)

=== Ionic and Atomic Radii

- Cations are smaller than their neutral, parent atoms.
- Anions are larger than their neutral, parent atoms.

=== Transition Metal Ions

- Transition metal ions rarely attain a noble gas electron configuration
  - requires the gain, or loss, of too many electrons
- In general, transition metal atoms tend to form more than one cation by losing all of their $n$s electrons and some of their $(n-1)d$ electrons
- In the first row of transition metal atoms (period 4), there are two exceptions to the usual way of writing electron configurations

$
    ce("Cr"): & [ce("Ar")]4s^1 3d^5 \
    ce("Cu"): & [ce("Ar")]4s^1 3d^10 \
  ce("Cu+2"): & [ce("Ar")]3d^9
$

